STRUCTURE OF THE links.csv FILE

Three columns, one row per MovieLens movie.
It is a "bridge" table between three different websites:
it links the ratings (ratings.csv, which use the MovieLens id) to the movies (movies_metadata.csv,
which uses the TMDB id).

    movieId,	 imdbId,	tmdbId
    1			,0114709,	862          <- Toy Story

- movieId
	Identifier of the movie on MovieLens: it is the same movieId as in ratings.csv

- imdbId
	Identifier of the movie on IMDb, WITHOUT the "tt" prefix and with leading zeros (0114709).
	In movies_metadata the same code is written with the prefix: tt0114709.
	It must be read as text: converting it to a number loses the leading zeros.

	it is actually not very useful

- tmdbId
	Identifier of the movie on TMDB: it is the same id as in movies_metadata, credits and keywords

    ratings.movieId -> links.movieId -> links.tmdbId -> movies_metadata.id

links_small.csv has the same structure and is the reduced version to use with ratings_small.csv.





FINDINGS FROM THE EDA SCRIPTS


- Structure (section 1):
	links.csv: 45,843 rows; links_small.csv: 9,125 rows.
	All values are digits only. imdbId always has 7 digits.
	movieId is never repeated and is sorted (from 1 to 176,279).


- Missing tmdbId (section 2):
	219 movieIds (0.48%) have no tmdbId, so their ratings cannot be linked to any movie.
	They are 13,503 ratings out of 26,024,289 (0.05%).
	In links_small 13 tmdbIds are missing.

	should we remove them?

- tmdbId used by several movieIds (section 3):
	29 tmdbIds are assigned to more than one movieId (59 movieIds in total, 30 more than needed).
	In all 29 cases the movieIds have DIFFERENT imdbIds: for MovieLens they are different movies,
	but they are linked to the same TMDB movie.

	Comparing with the imdb_id of movies_metadata, in each of the 29 groups exactly one movieId
	has the same IMDb code (29 correct) and the others do not (30 wrong).
	e.g. tmdbId 105045 (The Promise): movieId 690 (imdb 0111613, same as the metadata) and
	    movieId 1533 (imdb 0117398, different).
	-> 30 movieIds point to the wrong movie: their 9,453 ratings (0.04%) would end up on the wrong movie.

	These 29 tmdbIds are EXACTLY the 29 duplicated ids of movies_metadata (there too 59 rows, 30 extra).
	This suggests that the dataset was built by downloading from TMDB the data of every row of links:
	a tmdbId present twice in links produced two identical rows in movies_metadata.
	So the duplicates of movies_metadata (and of credits/keywords) are not different movies, but the effect
	of this linking error.


- Link with movies_metadata (section 4):
	164 tmdbIds do not match any valid movie of movies_metadata (29,219 ratings, 0.11%).
	3 of these are the movies with the broken row (Midnight Man, Mardock Scramble, Avalanche Sharks):
	rebuilding those rows recovers them.
	All the valid movies of movies_metadata have a row in links.

	For 95 movies the imdb id of links is different from the one in movies_metadata (same tmdbId):
	30 are the wrong movieIds of the previous point, the other 65 are individual cases
	(e.g. Two Friends, Venice, The Sleepover): one of the two files has the wrong IMDb code,
	and from the data it is impossible to tell which.
	-> links must be made on tmdbId, not on imdbId.


- Link with the ratings (section 5):
	All the movieIds rated in ratings.csv are in links.csv; 728 movieIds of links have no ratings.
	All the movieIds rated in ratings_small.csv are in links_small.csv; 59 movieIds of links_small have no ratings.


- links_small compared to links (section 6):
	links_small is NOT an exact subset of links: 10 movieIds are only in links_small.
	For the 9,115 movieIds present in both, imdbId and tmdbId are identical.
	-> links_small is fine for development, but the final version must use links.csv.


